  "meta": {
    "title": "Sort the letters: every a before every b",
    "blurb": "Find the first “ba”, turn it into “ab”, go back to the start, and repeat — until a scan reaches the end without finding one. Bubble sort, one swap per trip, with nothing but a head and five states.",
    "inputs": [
      {
        "w": "ba",
        "expect": "accept",
        "tape": "ab"
      },
      {
        "w": "bba",
        "expect": "accept",
        "tape": "abb"
      },
      {
        "w": "babab",
        "expect": "accept",
        "tape": "aabbb"
      },
      {
        "w": "bbbaaa",
        "expect": "accept",
        "tape": "aaabbb"
      },
      {
        "w": "aabb",
        "expect": "accept",
        "label": "already sorted",
        "tape": "aabb"
      },
      {
        "w": "",
        "expect": "accept",
        "label": "ε",
        "tape": ""
      }
    ],
    "library": {
      "author": {
        "login": "thethinkmachine"
      },
      "license": "CC-BY-4.0",
      "tags": [
        "sorting",
        "function",
        "time-complexity"
      ],
      "difficulty": "intro",
      "readme": "The machine never counts anything. It only knows one local rule — **ba → ab** — and applies it until it no longer applies. Every swap moves one a one cell to the left, so the number of swaps is the number of *inversions* (pairs b…a in the wrong order), and each is followed by a walk back to the start: O(n²) swaps, O(n³) steps in the worst case.\n\nIt always accepts. The answer is the tape: **babab** halts with **aabbb** on it. The card's examples list what each input leaves behind."
    }
  }
}
